1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,070
If you pressed these pads

2
00:00:01,070 --> 00:00:02,310
against a glass wall,

3
00:00:02,310 --> 00:00:03,180
you could lift your feet

4
00:00:03,180 --> 00:00:04,120
off the ground—

5
00:00:04,120 --> 00:00:06,080
and climb it like Spider-Man.

6
00:00:06,080 --> 00:00:07,410
Their surfaces are covered

7
00:00:07,410 --> 00:00:09,110
in microscopic wedges.

8
00:00:09,110 --> 00:00:10,290
As you pull downward,

9
00:00:10,290 --> 00:00:11,350
the wedges bend flat

10
00:00:11,350 --> 00:00:12,640
against the glass.

11
00:00:12,640 --> 00:00:14,050
This brings them so close

12
00:00:14,050 --> 00:00:15,780
that tiny molecular attractions

13
00:00:15,780 --> 00:00:17,610
can hold your entire weight.

14
00:00:17,610 --> 00:00:19,320
Special springs spread the load,

15
00:00:19,320 --> 00:00:20,350
so one little patch

16
00:00:20,350 --> 00:00:22,150
doesn’t have to hold you up alone.

17
00:00:22,150 --> 00:00:23,460
Then, you shift your weight

18
00:00:23,460 --> 00:00:24,870
onto the other pad.

19
00:00:24,870 --> 00:00:26,060
The first releases,

20
00:00:26,060 --> 00:00:26,600
letting you reach

21
00:00:26,600 --> 00:00:28,570
higher and repeat.

22
00:00:28,570 --> 00:00:29,800
But there’s a catch.

23
00:00:29,800 --> 00:00:30,990
They need a smooth surface

24
00:00:30,990 --> 00:00:31,800
to grip.

25
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:33,340
So you can climb glass—

26
00:00:33,340 --> 00:00:34,700
but switch to rough brick,

27
00:00:34,700 --> 00:00:36,080
and those microscopic wedges

28
00:00:36,080 --> 00:00:37,860
can’t make the same contact.

